  "tours": [
    {
      "id": "tour:retry",
      "title": "How a failed job gets retried",
      "summary": "This Go job runner keeps jobs in an in-memory queue and runs each one on a worker. When an attempt fails, the runner puts the job back with a delay that doubles each time. When the last of `retry.maxRetries` retries fails too, the runner parks the job in a dead-letter list. This tour follows one failed job and leaves out metrics and the worker pool.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "t1",
          "view": "view:retry-code",
          "focus": ["grp:retry-engine"],
          "note": "### Two files handle a failed job\n\nStart at the end of `Runner.Dispatch`: there the runner chooses between a retry and the dead-letter list. The queue then does what the runner asks.",
          "code": [
            {
              "file": "internal/runner/runner.go",
              "symbol": "Runner.Dispatch",
              "span": {
                "from": 33,
                "to": 46
              },
              "role": "definition"
            },
            {
              "file": "internal/queue/queue.go",
              "symbol": "Queue.Requeue",
              "role": "definition"
            }
          ],
          "editor": {
            "primary": "internal/runner/runner.go"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "t2",
          "view": "view:retry",
          "focus": ["retry:2"],
          "note": "### Each pass of the loop runs one attempt\n\n`Worker.Run` waits for the handler or for the timeout, whichever comes first. A failure is returned in `Result.Err`, not as an error, and the runner checks it next.",
          "code": [
            {
              "file": "internal/runner/runner.go",
              "symbol": "Runner.Dispatch",
              "find": "result := w.Run(",
              "role": "call-site"
            },
            {
              "file": "internal/worker/worker.go",
              "symbol": "Worker.Run",
              "span": {
